            <ArticleTitle>Fragment Pharmacophore-Based Screening: An Efficient Approach for Discovery of New Inhibitors of Toll-Like Receptor 5.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>834-840</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2174/1386207319666160907103507</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM AND OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA) is a progressing autoimmune inflammatory disease of joint, hallmarked by inflammation, pain and atrophy of bones. Toll-like receptor 5 (TLR5) is a novel inflammatory mediator in RA, and TLR5 inhibitors are speculated to have a therapeutic potential for the treatment of RA.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Here we applied fragment pharmacophore-based virtual screening to identify novel TLR5 ligands.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Among compounds collected from Otava peptidomimetic compounds, Maybridge fragment and ZINC libraries, 3355 compounds were selected for docking into the flagellin-binding site of TLR5. 16 compounds with the required interaction, critical amino acid residues and the binding free energies &lt;-7 kcal/mol were identified as potential TLR5 inhibitors, one of which was followed up by molecular dynamics simulation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">These compounds open a possibility to discover novel TLR5 inhibitors for the treatment of RA.</AbstractText>
